### Justification
The claim is clearly refuted by official data and government statements. India's imports from China actually **decreased by 27.63%** (to $21.58 billion from ~$29.82 billion the previous year) during April–August 2020, not increased. The 27% increase figure actually referred to India's **exports** to China, not imports. Commerce Minister Piyush Goyal confirmed the decline in Parliament, and [PIB Fact Check](https://www.oneindia.com/fact-check/fake-india-s-import-to-china-has-not-increased-by-27-per-cent-3165856.html) officially labeled the claim as "#Fake" on October 20, 2020. The original source was a [tweet by journalist Pankaj Pachauri](https://www.oneindia.com/fact-check/fake-india-s-import-to-china-has-not-increased-by-27-per-cent-3165856.html), which confused or swapped the export and import figures. The decline was partly attributed to [COVID-19-related supply and demand disruptions](https://psuwatch.com/national-news/india-imports-china-decline-160-60-billion-april-july-piyush-goyal), and affected categories included electronic components, telecom instruments, computer hardware, and industrial machinery.